data.content:
**What happened:** This is a C# 14 breaking change — **`Span<T>` overloads are now applicable in more scenarios**.

When you write `array.Contains(num)` on an `int[]`, C# 14's new span conversions cause the compiler to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(Span<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. The `MemoryExtensions` overload is **not representable in expression trees**, so it throws at runtime when the expression tree is compiled/interpreted.

**How to fix — cast the array to `IEnumerable<T>`:**

```csharp
// Before (breaks in .NET 10 / C# 14)
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.Contains(num);

// Fix: force Enumerable.Contains via IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Alternative: use Enumerable explicitly
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Both approaches force the compiler to resolve `Enumerable.Contains`, which is expression-tree-compatible.
